window.EXAMS = {"2021":{"year":2021,"listening":[{"title":"1. Tick the right option.","items":[{"id":"1","label":"The podcast generally deals with people …","type":"choice","options":["who try to fix local problems.","who travel to fix problems in the world.","who want to find global solutions to world-wide problems.","who try to solve general problems they are confronted with in their communities."],"correct":[3],"points":1}]},{"title":"2. “Little Free Library”. Complete the table.","items":[{"id":"2a","label":"kind of organization","key":"non-profit (organization) / (with) no profit","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2b","label":"basic idea (be precise!)","key":"Anyone can take a book or leave a book / exchange books. Nur „take“ oder nur „leave“ genügt nicht.","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"3. Gapped summary. Fill in the missing words and expressions.","items":[{"id":"3a","label":"Brighton, a town on the ___ of the UK.","key":"south-east coast; nur south oder east genügt nicht.","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"3b","label":"It is set up especially for ___ .","key":"children / kids","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"3c","label":"It is located in a park, in the middle of a ___ .","key":"square","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"3d","label":"Users don’t have to ___ , just close the door.","key":"sign / do anything","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"4. The “Little Free Library” in Brighton. Tick the two correct statements.","items":[{"id":"4","label":"Select two statements.","type":"choice","options":["Michelle Pauli is a frequent user of the “Little Free Library”.","Many locals participated in creating the “Little Free Library”.","A professional woodworker designed the “Little Free Library”.","The “Little Free Library” is inside a beach hut to protect it against the sea.","There are always enough books in the “Little Free Library”."],"correct":[1,4],"points":2}]},{"title":"5. The “Little Free Library” in Brighton.","items":[{"id":"5a1","label":"First reason why it reaches all kinds of people","key":"Zwei verschiedene aus: many different people walk by; direct access from main road/beach; next to a bus stop.","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"5a2","label":"Second reason why it reaches all kinds of people","key":"Zweiter eigenständiger Grund aus: many different people walk by; direct access from main road/beach; next to a bus stop.","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"5b","label":"Time it has been open now","key":"(just over) 1 year","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"5c","label":"Feedback from users","key":"positive / people love it","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"5d1","label":"First fear the organizers had had","key":"Zwei verschiedene aus: inappropriate things left there; people lose interest; vandalism / repairs; frequent restocking.","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"5d2","label":"Second fear the organizers had had","key":"Zweiter eigenständiger Grund aus den genannten. „System doesn’t work“ ist zu allgemein.","type":"text","points":1}]}],"grammar":[{"id":"1","answers":["has been"],"type":"correction","original":"is","explanation":"For many years reicht hier bis in die Gegenwart: present perfect.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"2","answers":["fast"],"type":"correction","original":"fastly","explanation":"Fast bleibt als Adverb unverändert.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"3","answers":["1,700"],"type":"correction","original":"1.700","explanation":"Im Englischen trennt das Komma die Tausender.","topic":"possessive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"4","answers":["worse"],"type":"correction","original":"worser","explanation":"Unregelmäßiger Komparativ: bad → worse.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"5","answers":["in spite of","despite"],"type":"correction","original":"in spite","explanation":"Vor einer Nomengruppe: despite oder in spite of.","topic":"prepositions","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"6","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"took part","explanation":"Abgeschlossener Zeitpunkt: simple past.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"7","answers":["a teacher"],"type":"correction","original":"teacher","explanation":"Berufsangaben benötigen den unbestimmten Artikel.","topic":"quantifiers","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"8","answers":["would not have cancelled","wouldn’t have cancelled","would not have canceled","wouldn’t have canceled"],"type":"correction","original":"hadn’t cancelled","explanation":"Typ III: would not have + past participle.","topic":"conditionals","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"9","answers":["for"],"type":"correction","original":"since","explanation":"16 Jahre sind eine Zeitspanne → for.","topic":"prepositions","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"10","answers":["students’"],"type":"correction","original":"students","explanation":"Regelmäßiger Plural auf s: Apostroph nach dem s.","topic":"possessive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"11","answers":["any"],"type":"correction","original":"some","explanation":"Im verneinten Satz steht hier any.","topic":"quantifiers","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"12","answers":["from playing"],"type":"correction","original":"to play","explanation":"Keep someone from + -ing.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"13","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"asking","explanation":"Er erinnert sich an eine vergangene Handlung: remember + -ing.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"14","answers":["would help"],"type":"correction","original":"will help","explanation":"Will → would nach told.","topic":"reported","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"15","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"at","explanation":"Feste Verbindung: good/better at.","topic":"prepositions","caseSensitive":false}],"passages":[{"kind":"correction","title":"High school football in New Jersey","text":"New Jersey {{1|is}} one of the top high school football states for many years, sending stars to top college programs and the N.F.L. Now the number of teenagers who are taking part in high school football is falling really {{2|fastly}}. Football coaches across New Jersey are asking the same question: Where have all the players gone? The latest active participation numbers presented in August showed a serious statewide decline: The last high school football season saw nearly {{3|1.700}} fewer players than the year before. Only three other states were even {{4|worser}} affected. Many coaches said that {{5|in spite}} New Jersey’s proud high school football tradition the main reason was a growing worry about head injuries. Football remains the nation’s most popular sport. But since 2009, when a record 1 million athletes {{6|took part}} , interest has fallen. Coach Mike Warnock, who is also {{7|teacher}}, does not need national numbers to understand what is happening at his school: If there had been enough players for the last game, they {{8|hadn’t cancelled}} a game for the first time in their 90 - year-long history. In his Math class {{9|since}} the past 16 years, Warnock has handed out a questionnaire each school year to learn more about his {{10|students}} interests. He studies these forms, finds out about students who are not playing sports and draws their attention to high school football: “I’m like, ‘Hey, you don’t play {{11|some}} sports this season, do you? What’s keeping you {{12|to play}} football?’” Another coach remembers {{13|asking}} a parent for weeks to let her son play. He told her that football {{14|will help}} him a lot to get better {{15|at}} his other sport, baske tball. “If I can win over the mother,” he says, “it’s pretty easy after that.”","offset":0}],"writingKey":"Folgende inhaltliche Punkte sollten in der Schülerarbeit enthalten sein: \nAbout the programme “Classroom under Sail”: \n 34 students in Year 10\n six-month journey on a sailing ship across the Atlantic (including classes)\n 75 days on land, opportunities to climb mountains, sleep in the jungle, get to\nknow native people\nHow to participate: \n write an application (personal statement/letter of motivation)\n 50 applicants chosen to show their skills during a trial week (team spirit,\nenthusiasm, open-mindedness)\nAbout the daily routine on board: \n (three weeks to get used to life on board)\n two groups take turns on a daily basis: one group helps run the ship, the\nother group has got lessons\nWhat to do without: \n very limited luggage, no luxury goods\n mobile phones handed over (only returned on land to get in touch with\nparents/ friends)\n (parents)\n4 BE","writingSource":"Jahrgangsstufentest 10 Englisch 2021 – Gymnasium: Aufgaben   \n 4 \nPart III: Sprachmittlung 20 BE \n \nIhre Schule nimmt an einem EU -Wettbewerb zum Thema Exploring the world  teil. \nVerfassen Sie auf Basis des vorliegenden Textes einen englischsprachigen Artikel \n(Umfang ca. 1 60 Wörter), der das Projekt „Klassenzimmer unter Segeln“ vorstellt.  \nErläutern Sie die Grundzüge dieses Projekts und beschreiben Sie, wie man daran \nteilnehmen kann, wie der Alltag auf dem Schiff aussieht und auf was man bereit sein muss \nzu verzichten. Finden Sie eine treffende Überschrift.  \n \nKLASSENZIMMER UNTER SEGELN \n \nSchülerin Anna (15) war wie gebannt, als ihr im Juli 2016 ein Zeitungsartikel in die Hände fiel, in dem \nes um das Programm „Klassenzimmer unter Segeln“ ging: 34 Schüler der zehnten Jahrgangsst ufe \nfolgen auf einem Segelschiff den Spuren großer Entdecker. Sechs Monate, in denen die Jugendlichen \naus ganz Deutschland in Begleitung von Profis nicht nur den Atlantik überqueren, sondern auch Berge \nbesteigen, im Dschungel übernachten und Ureinwohner ke nnenlernen. „Ich war total fasziniert“, \nberichtet Anna.  \nZwischen 150 und 200 Schüler haben sich für die 34 Plätze bei der Expedition, die von der Friedrich -\nAlexander-Universität Erlangen-Nürnberg durchgeführt wird, beworben. Ein großer Ansturm auf die \nThor Heyerdahl, so heißt das Segelschiff. Doch mit ihrem Motivationsschreiben hat Anna die \nOrganisatoren von sich überzeugen können. Sie war unter den 50 Auserwählten, die zu einer \nProbewoche eingeladen wurden.  \nNeben den Schülern gehören zur Schiffsmannschaf t auch fünf Lehrer. Der Rest sind seebewährte \nProfis, neben dem Kapitän unter anderem auch ein Bootsmann und ein Bordarzt. Insgesamt besteht \ndie Mannschaft aus 50 Personen – darunter auch Anna. Sie wurde in das Projekt aufgenommen, weil \nsie in der Probewoc he zeigte, dass sie die erforderlichen Voraussetzungen mitbrachte. Teamfähig \nmussten die Bewerber sein, begeisterungsfähig, neugierig und aufgeschlossen für Neues. \nAuf dem Segeltörn sind auch 75 Landtage eingeplant. Die Schüler legen zum Beispiel auf den \nBermudas an. Besonders aber freut sich Anna auf die San -Blas-Inseln, die vor Panama liegen. „Die \nsind der Prototyp der einsamen Insel“, berichtet Anna. Und was nimmt sie mit auf ihre einsame Insel? \nDa muss Anna keine Sekunde nachdenken. „Meine Kamera – unbedingt!“ Auf vieles andere muss die \n15-Jährige hingegen verzichten. Neben einem Tagesrucksack darf sie einen 100 -Liter-Seesack mit an \nBord nehmen. Von den Organisatoren gibt es eine Packliste, daran können sich die Nachwuchs -\nSegler orientieren. Ganz o ben dabei: Ölzeug. „Das sind ganz dicke und  robuste Regenjacken und       \n-hosen.“ Viel Platz für Luxus bleibt nicht im Gepäck. Die Jugendlichen müssen auf hoher See ohne \nMobiltelefone auskommen. So steht es im Bordregelregister. „Die Handys werden eingesa mmelt. Erst \nwenn wir an Land gehen und uns dort akklimatisiert haben, bekommen wir sie wieder, damit wir auch \nunsere Eltern und Freunde kontaktieren können“, berichtet Anna. Die ersten drei Wochen haben die \nSchüler Zeit, um sich auf dem Schiff einzuleben u nd an den Bordalltag zu gewöhnen. Danach werden \nzwei Gruppen gebildet, die abwechselnd je einen Tag am Schiffsbetrieb und am Unterricht \nteilnehmen. So bekommen die Matrosen etwa 40 Prozent des normalen Schulstoffs mit. Freilich lernt \nAnna auf der Reise une ndlich viel mehr. Allein an Bord werden Praktika beim Bootsmann und \nProviantmeister durchlaufen. \nAm 14. Oktober läuft die Thor Heyerdahl aus. Die Vorfreude steigt, aber auch erste Bedenken machen \nsich bei Anna breit. War es die richtige Entscheidung? Und w ie hart wird die Trennung von den \nEltern? Da hilft es, dass Mama und Papa zu 100 Prozent hinter der Entscheidung ihrer Tochter \nstehen. „Das ist ein sehr gutes Gefühl“, betont Anna. „Trotzdem werden beim Abschied bestimmt ein \npaar Tränen fließen.“ \nFrom: https://www.merkur.de/lokales/freising/freising-ort28692/anna-hisst-segel-freisinger-schuelerin-fuer-sechs-monate-\nauf-hoher-see-10322205.html (adapted; last visited: June 12, 2019) \n(Inhalt ____/ 4 BE + Sprache ____/ 6 BE) x 2 = _____ / 20 BE \n \nTotal: _____ / 50 BE"},"2022":{"year":2022,"listening":[{"title":"1. Complete the information on Jill Heinerth.","items":[{"id":"1a","label":"year of Jill’s first iceberg dive","key":"2001","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"1b","label":"nationality","key":"Canadian","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"1c","label":"Jill is a diver and …","key":"(underwater) filmmaker","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"2. Facts on the iceberg. Complete the table.","items":[{"id":"2a","label":"name","key":"B 15","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2b","label":"size","key":"(comparable to the size of) Jamaica","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2c","label":"location","key":"Antarctic","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"3. Jill Heinerth’s worries. Fill in the missing information.","items":[{"id":"3a","label":"Her greatest worry was that the water was ___ and the team would not be able to come to the surface in an emergency.","key":"below zero / −1.8 degrees / too or very cold / freezing cold","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"3b","label":"She was the only one of them who had ___ .","key":"cold water experience","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"4. Name two items of their diving equipment.","items":[{"id":"4a","label":"First item","key":"dry/wet suits ODER lights / flashlights / torches","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"4b","label":"Second item","key":"Der jeweils andere Gegenstand: dry/wet suits ODER lights / flashlights / torches. Keine doppelte Wertung.","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"5. Jill Heinerth’s impression of the ice cave. Tick the two correct answers.","items":[{"id":"5","label":"It was …","type":"choice","options":["silent.","scary.","lonely.","colourful.","surprising."],"correct":[3,4],"points":2}]},{"title":"6. Tick the correct answer.","items":[{"id":"6","label":"Forms of life Jill Heinerth found down there:","type":"choice","options":["fish","corals","spider-like creatures"],"correct":[2],"points":1}]},{"title":"7. Lessons that Jill Heinerth has learned from diving in the polar regions.","items":[{"id":"7","label":"Tick the two correct answers. Jill Heinerth …","type":"choice","options":["takes fewer risks when diving.","has become a political person.","has stopped going there to protect nature.","wants to draw attention to how the area is changing.","wants people to go there and experience the area themselves."],"correct":[0,3],"points":2}]}],"grammar":[{"id":"1","answers":["British"],"type":"correction","original":"british","explanation":"Nationalitäten werden großgeschrieben.","topic":"basics","caseSensitive":true},{"id":"2","answers":["fastest"],"type":"correction","original":"most fast","explanation":"Superlativ nach the: fastest.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"3","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"finishing","explanation":"Nach before kann eine verkürzte -ing-Konstruktion stehen.","topic":"participles","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"4","answers":["During","Throughout","In"],"type":"correction","original":"While","explanation":"Vor der Nomengruppe steht eine Präposition.","topic":"prepositions","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"5","answers":["just wanted"],"type":"correction","original":"had just wanted","explanation":"Ohne Vorvergangenheitsbezug: simple past, nicht had just wanted.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"6","answers":["by"],"type":"correction","original":"from","explanation":"By nennt im Passiv den Handelnden.","topic":"passive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"7","answers":["to complete"],"type":"correction","original":"of completing","explanation":"Be able + to-infinitive.","topic":"modals","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"8","answers":["have finished"],"type":"correction","original":"finished","explanation":"Since 2003 bis jetzt → present perfect.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"9","answers":["which"],"type":"correction","original":"that","explanation":"Non-defining relative clause nach Komma: which.","topic":"relatives","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"10","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"were flown","explanation":"Past participle im Passiv: flown.","topic":"passive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"11","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"had been","explanation":"Vorzeitigkeit in indirekter Rede: past perfect.","topic":"reported","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"12","answers":["little"],"type":"correction","original":"few","explanation":"Sleep ist nicht zählbar.","topic":"quantifiers","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"13","answers":["wouldn’t have won","would not have won"],"type":"correction","original":"hadn’t won","explanation":"Typ III: would have + won.","topic":"conditionals","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"14","answers":["Most"],"type":"correction","original":"The most","explanation":"Bei allgemeinen Aussagen: most people ohne the.","topic":"quantifiers","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"15","answers":["well","a lot"],"type":"correction","original":"good","explanation":"Adverb von good: well.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false}],"passages":[{"kind":"correction","title":"Seven marathons in seven days","text":"What started out as a ”crazy ambition” yesterday ended in a world record as the {{1|british}} runner Susannah Gill successfully completed seven marathons across seven continents in seven days to win the World Marathon Challenge in the {{2|most fast}} time by a woman athlete. Along the way Gill, a 34 -year-old from London, braved -35°C temperatures in Antarctica and 35 °C heat in Cape Town before {{3|finishing}} the event in Miami in a total time of 24 hours 19 minutes and nine seconds. {{4|While}} the week-long challenge, she won every race, except in Antarctica where she came second. “It was a crazy ambition but the challenge seemed absolutely fascinating,” she admitted. “Ten years ago, I {{5|had just wanted}} to get fit and run the London Marathon. Now, marathon running has literally taken me around the world.” Participants are warned in advance {{6|from}} the race organisers that they must be in “excellent health and able {{7|of completing}} the World Marathon Challenge”. However, fewer than 200 people {{8|finished}} the annual “777” marathon since Sir Ranulph Fiennes first achieved the feat 1 in 2003. The £31,500 entry fee, {{9|that}} pays for logistics and accommodation, is also a big factor in that. Gill and the other 39 competitors {{10|were flown}} more than 55,000 miles across the globe on chartered planes. Afterwards, Gill admitted that it {{11|had been}} hard running seven marathons in the space of a week with very {{12|few}} sleep. “ If I hadn’t trained for the marathons every day, I’m sure I {{13|hadn’t won}} it,” she explained. “{{14|The most}} people don’t know that you don’t only have to practise running, you also have to make sure that you eat {{15|good}} to get enough calories in.” “Now I can get back to normal life but I’m sure I’ll have some aching muscles,” she added.","offset":0}],"writingKey":"Folgende inhaltliche Punkte sollten in der Schülerarbeit enthalten sein: \n \nAbout the project “Donate your deposit - give us the mone y from your returnable \nbottles”: \n containers are located at the security control \n passengers can dump the bottles they are not allowed to take on the plane \n workers take care of the containers and sort the bottles and cans \nIts advantages: \n great idea how to deal with the  huge number of bottles which fill the bins  (at \nHamburg Airport: half a million plastic bottles each year) \n bottles are returned or recycled instead of burning them \n money from the deposit is used to create new jobs (three in Hamburg alone) and \nto pay the workers that are in charge of the bottles \n win-win situation: good for the airport, the environment and the community \nHow Uwe Tröger’s life changed: \n Uwe Tröger is one of the people in charge of the bottle-collection in Hamburg \n he had lost his job and started collecting bottles in the street \n the project provides a regular job (at minimum wa ge), a daily routine and a \nmeaningful task \n \n4 BE","writingSource":"5 \nJahrgangsstufentest 10 Englisch 2022 – Gymnasium: Aufgaben \n \n \n \n \n \nIhre AG Umweltschutz nimmt am EU -Projekt “Initiatives to  save our planet” teil. Als eine \nVorzeigeaktion stellen Sie die Initiative \"Donate your deposit - give us the money from your \nreturnable bottles\" des Hamburger Flughafens auf der englischsprachigen EU -Projekt-\nWebseite vor.   \nSchreiben Sie einen Artikel (ca . 160 Wörter) für diese Webseite, in welchem Sie die \nFunktionsweise des Projekts erklären und dessen Vorteile schildern. Zeigen Sie dabei auch \nauf, wie das Projekt das Leben von Uwe Tröger verändert hat.   \n \nHerrn Uwes Gespür für Flaschen \nJedes Jahr werden am Hamburger Flughafen mehr als eine halbe Million Plastikflaschen \nweggeworfen. Dass diese nicht im Müll landen, dafür sorgt das Projekt „Spende dein Pfand“. \nEs gibt so viel zu fühlen bei einer Flasche. Da ist die Größe, das Material – ist sie aus Glas oder \nKunststoff, hat sie dünne oder dicke Wände, und vor allem: welche Form? Uwe Tröger erkennt \n„seine Flaschen“ sogar im Dunkeln. Die, mit denen sich Geld verdiene n lässt, die dünnwandigen \nPfandflaschen aus Plastik. Da sind bis zu 25 Cent p ro Stück drin. „Das sind die wichtigsten, die \nheiligen Flaschen“, sagt er. \nFür die einen sind Flaschen Abfall. Weg damit, in den nächsten Mülleimer, am Flughafen etwa, \nbevor es durch die Sicherheitsschleuse geht und man ohnehin alle Flüssigkeiten abgeben m uss. \nFür Uwe Tröger ist so eine Flasche Teil seines Einkommens. Der 50 -Jährige arbeitet am \nHamburger Flughafen für das Projekt „Spende dein Pfand“ als Wertstoffbeauftragter, so heißt \nseine Position dort.  Pfandsammler möchte er nicht genannt werden. „ Das klingt ja so, als hätte \nich keinen Job. “ Den hat er aber. Uwe Tröger ist fest angestellt, er bezieht Mindestlohn, sein \nArbeitsvertrag ist unbefristet. Er lebt von dem, was andere Menschen wegwerfen: Plastikflaschen \nund Pfanddosen. \n„Spende dein Pfand “ startete im September 2015 in Zusammenarbeit mit dem Flughafen und \ndem Straßenmagazin „Hinz&Kunzt“. Uwe Tröger war von Anfang an dabei. Das Prinzip ist \neinfach: Durchsichtige Plastiktonnen stehen vor den Sicherheitskontrollen des Flughafens. \nPassagiere können darin ihre Pfandflaschen entsorgen. Ist eine Tonne voll, kommt Tröger mit \neiner Sackkarre vorb ei, aufladen, in ein Nebengebäude karren, sortieren. „Das ist dann ein \nbisschen so, als würde ich Fässer mit Geld durch die Gegend rollen. “ Gelegentlich steht er vor \nden Sicherheitskontrollen der Business -Class. Geschäftsreisende, die es eilig haben, könne n \nhier schneller einchecken. Manchmal, sagt Tröger, wundere er sich schon, was dort alles in den \nTonnen lande: Teure Gesichtscremes, verschlossene Cola-Dosen, sogar Essen. \nDas Projekt entstand aus einer Notlage heraus: Gesucht wurde eine Lösung für die Pfa ndflut am \nFlughafen. Pro Tag reisen hier Zehntausende Passagiere ab und hinterlassen ihre Wasser - und \nLimoflaschen. Früher landeten die einfach im Restmüll, kamen in die Müllverbrennungsanlage, \nwurden verheizt. Nun finanziert der Pfanderlös dreieinhalb Arbeitsplätze – drei Pfandbeauftragte \nund eine studentische Hilfskraft. Allein im vergangenen Jahr sammelte das Team über eine halbe \nMillion Flaschen, die jetzt auch nicht mehr die Abfalleimer verstopfen. Win-win, würde man in der \nWirtschaft sagen.  \nUwe Tröger kam sozusagen als Experte dazu. Er hielt sich mit Gelegenheitsjobs über Wasser, \nhalf bei Umzügen. Dann wurde er arbeitslos. Er hat schon früher Pfandflaschen gesammelt – an \nden Landungsbrücken am Hamburger Hafen, einem Hotspot für Touristen. „Das ging zu der Zeit \nnoch ganz gut“, erinnert er sich. Mittlerweile seien aber viele Sammler unterwegs, das Geschäft \nlohne sich kaum noch. Die Flut von  Pfandsammlern sei ein Zeichen von Armut, sagt  Stephan \nKarrenbauer, Sozialpädagoge beim Straßenmagazin „Hinz&Kunzt“: „Man kann heute nicht mehr \nsagen: Das machen nur die Obdachlosen “, erklärt er, „Heute sammeln auch Rentner, Früh -\nRentner oder Osteuropäer.“ Nicht bei allen stehe dabei der Geldaspekt im Vordergrund. „Einige \nsehnen sich auch nach einem geregelten Tagesablauf, nach einer sinnvollen Aufgabe. Einfach \nmal wieder unter Menschen zu kommen.“ \nFrom: https://www.stern.de/wirtschaft/muell/spende-dein-pfand--unterwegs-mit-einem-pfandsammler-am-flughafen-\n7865262.html (483 words, adapted, last visited: August 20, 2020) \n \n(Inhalt ____ / 4 BE + Sprache ____ / 6 BE) x 2 = ______ / 20 BE \n \nTotal: ______ / 50 BE \nPart III: Sprachmittlung    20 BE"},"2023":{"year":2023,"listening":[{"title":"1. Fill in the missing information on the Golden Gate Bridge.","items":[{"id":"1a","label":"A problem before building:","key":"strong winds / rough currents","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"1b","label":"The bridge was built over …","key":"San Francisco Bay / Golden Gate Strait","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"1c","label":"Opening date:","key":"May 27, 1937","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"1d","label":"Length:","key":"almost 2 miles","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"2. Gapped summary. Complete the text.","items":[{"id":"2a","label":"On opening day, people were only allowed to ___ .","key":"walk across the bridge / cross the bridge on foot","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2b","label":"The bridge has become one of the ___ constructions in the world.","key":"most photographed","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"3. Edgar Stone and the Golden Gate Bridge.","items":[{"id":"3a","label":"Reason why he and his friends hung out all night before the opening:","key":"They wanted to be the first in line to cross.","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"3b","label":"To him and his friends, crossing the bridge felt (one adjective):","key":"scary / frightening","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"4. Gapped summary. Complete the text.","items":[{"id":"4a","label":"Nancy realised that the walkway ___ .","key":"rose (under her feet) / went up(hill)","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"4b","label":"After the middle, it ___ again.","key":"levelled off / went down","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"5. Give two arguments against the Golden Gate Bridge according to Nancy Kent Danielson.","items":[{"id":"5a","label":"First argument","key":"Zwei verschiedene aus: spoil the beauty of nature; ruin Marin County; too many people.","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"5b","label":"Second argument","key":"Zweiter eigenständiger Grund aus: spoil the beauty of nature; ruin Marin County; too many people.","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"6. George Klein. Tick the two correct answers.","items":[{"id":"6","label":"On the first day the bridge was open, George Klein …","type":"choice","options":["was 59 years old.","ran because it was cold.","ran to the other end of the bridge.","was dressed in shorts despite the cold weather.","was welcomed by an official at the other side of the bridge."],"correct":[2,3],"points":2}]},{"title":"7. The ferry. Name one argument in favour of using it.","items":[{"id":"7","label":"One argument:","key":"commuting is still quicker / tourists admire scenery / priceless view of the bridge","type":"text","points":1}]}],"grammar":[{"id":"1","answers":["during","because of","in","throughout"],"type":"correction","original":"while","explanation":"Vor einer Nomengruppe: during.","topic":"prepositions","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"2","answers":["weren’t able","weren’t allowed","were not able","were not allowed"],"type":"correction","original":"didn’t have","explanation":"Die Möglichkeit fehlte: were not able, nicht did not have to.","topic":"modals","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"3","answers":["getting"],"type":"correction","original":"to get","explanation":"Stop + -ing: Sie bekamen kein kostenloses Material mehr.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"4","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"were delivered","explanation":"Plural-Passiv der Vergangenheit: were delivered.","topic":"passive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"5","answers":["a librarian"],"type":"correction","original":"librarian","explanation":"Berufsbezeichnung mit a.","topic":"quantifiers","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"6","answers":["has been working"],"type":"correction","original":"is working","explanation":"Anhaltende Tätigkeit bis jetzt: present perfect progressive.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"7","answers":["closed","was closed","had to close"],"type":"correction","original":"has closed","explanation":"Abgeschlossener Zeitpunkt → simple past; auch Passiv ist möglich.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"8","answers":["her students to"],"type":"correction","original":"that her students","explanation":"Want + Person + to-infinitive.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"9","answers":["Passek’s"],"type":"correction","original":"Passeks","explanation":"Singular-Genitiv: Apostroph + s.","topic":"possessive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"10","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"had used","explanation":"Nutzung fand vor einem vergangenen Ereignis statt: past perfect.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"11","answers":["delivering"],"type":"correction","original":"deliver","explanation":"Nach Präposition for steht -ing.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"12","answers":["these","the","those"],"type":"correction","original":"this","explanation":"This → these im Plural.","topic":"quantifiers","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"13","answers":["flew"],"type":"correction","original":"flied","explanation":"Fly → flew → flown.","topic":"possessive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"14","answers":["get","receive"],"type":"correction","original":"become","explanation":"Get/receive bedeutet bekommen; become bedeutet werden.","topic":"possessive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"15","answers":["hadn’t","had not"],"type":"correction","original":"wouldn’t have","explanation":"Typ III: if + past perfect.","topic":"conditionals","caseSensitive":false}],"passages":[{"kind":"correction","title":"Library books by drone","text":"In the year 2020, {{1|while}} the Covid -19 pandemic, unfortunately, a very large number of American students {{2|didn’t have}} to visit the school libraries. So families stopped {{3|to get}} free reading material. Kids in the Montgomery County school district in Virginia, however, had a special way of getting books: they {{4|were delivered}} by drones. A drone is a small remote -controlled aircraft, like a toy-sized helicopter. Kelly Passek is {{5|librarian}} at Blacksburg Middle School. She {{6|is working}} there for over 15 years now. When Blacksburg Middle School {{7|has closed}} in the spring of 2020 because of Covid-19, she wanted {{8|that her students}} have access to books. In Kelly {{9|Passeks}} hometown, Wing Aviation de livers food and other items from lots of restaurants and stores. She {{10|had used}} the drone service before to have things {{11|deliver}}ed to her own home, and when the pandemic started over a year ago, she thought it could work for deliver books to students, too. The people at Wing agreed and the company began with the deliveries. Students who lived in the delivery zone could fill out a form to ask for books. Passek then put {{12|this}} books in a special box and took them to Wing’s shipping cente r. The drone {{13|flied}} to the delivery location, lowered the box on a cable, and released it close to the ground. Kelly Passek said that kids were thrilled to be the first in the world to {{14|become}} a library book by drone. If the school {{15|wouldn’t have}} used the system, the kids wouldn’t have received any books at all.","offset":0}],"writingKey":"Folgende inhaltliche Punkte sollten in der Schülerarbeit enthalten sein: \n \nGeneral information on the Federal Volunteer Service: \n• aimed at people who are at least 16 years old  \n• foreign citizens can also participate  \n• duration: generally twelve months (possibility to shorten, extend or even break off the \nservice)  \n• can be carried out in different areas (e.g. in a hospital or a cultural institution)  \n• pocket money (if paid) is set at a maximum rate of EUR 390 per month (expenses such as \ntravel costs may be covered as well) \n \nBenefits: \n• gain practical work experience for their later lives \n• helps to discover the ir personal strengths and to find out which professional tasks and \nactivities suit them best \n• they get involved for the common good \n• (flexibility: they can quit their service early) \n \nProblems: \n• one in three quits their service early  \n• reasons: \no some institutions exploit their volunteers and treat them unfairly (pay only low wages \nand volunteers have to perform unpleasant tasks, e.g. cleaning or taking the rubbish \nout) \no feeling they aren’t learning anything \n \n \n4 BE","writingSource":"5 \nJahrgangsstufentest 10 Englisch 2023 – Gymnasium: Aufgaben \n \n \n  \n \n \nYou are taking part in an international school project called “School’s over, what now?”, \nwhich deals with different possibilities for students after school. You have been asked to \ncontribute a text for its website about the Federal Volunteer Service in Germany.  \n \nOn the basis of the text below, write an article of about 180 words in which you give \ngeneral information on the Federal Volunteer Service, its benefits and problems.  \n \nDon’t forget to find a suitable headline for your article.  \n \nBufdi ist nicht gleich Bufdi! \n \nJede dritte Person, die einen Bundesfreiwilligendienst  (auch Bufdi genannt) macht, sich also für \neinen gewissen Zeitraum für die Allgemeinheit engagiert, bricht diesen vorzeitig ab. Aber woran \nliegt das? Die Suche nach den Ursachen ist nicht leicht , denn jeder Bundesfreiwilligendienst ist \nanders. Die Einsatzstellen können z. B. Krankenhäuser, Schulen, Altersheime, Vereine oder \nKultureinrichtungen, aber auch Bauernhöfe sein.  \nEinen Bufdi kann jede und jeder ab 16 Jahren machen, auch Ausländer können teilnehmen. Der \nDienst dauert in der Regel ein Jahr, kann aber auf sechs Monate verkürzt oder maximal auf 24 \nMonate verlängert werden. Da der Dienst freiwillig ist, muss er nicht vergütet werd en. Die \nEinsatzstellen können aber ein Taschengeld von höchstens 390 Euro festsetzen.  \nDeshalb unterscheiden sich die Umstände, unter denen die Menschen ihren Dienst leisten. Patrik \nSchnell begann seinen Bufdi in einer Kindertagesstätte – und brach ihn  nach zwei Wochen  ab. \n„Ich wollte ein wenig Berufserfahrung sammeln. Ich hatte vorher schon Praktika in derselben Kita \ngemacht.“ Doch als Bufdi war die Arbeit nicht mehr dieselbe. \n„Mir war klar, dass ich eine 40 -Stunden-Woche haben werde – und das für einen Lo hn von 250 \nEuro im Monat. Das war ok“, erzählt der 20 -Jährige heute, der Wirtschaftswissenschaften in \nKonstanz studiert. Das Problem war ein anderes: Die  Arbeit in der Kita  war nicht das, was er \nerwartet hatte.  „Ich musste vor allem putzen und aufräumen. G erade Aufgaben wie Müll \nwegbringen, 50 Lätzchen am Tag waschen und auf die Leine hängen, hätte man einfach auf alle \nMitarbeiter verteilen können, und nicht den  Bufdi alles machen lassen.“ Patrik hatte in der Kita \nnicht das Gefühl, wertgeschätzt und  fair behandelt zu werden. „ Manche Unternehmen glauben, \nsie können mit den Bufdis machen, was sie wollen. Sie setzen sie als volle Arbeitskräfte ein, \nentlohnen sie schlecht. Einen Lerneffekt gibt es nicht.“  \nGanz andere Erfahrungen hat Andreas Penzkofer gemacht. Der 20-Jährige aus München half als \nBufdi beim Erzbischöflichen Jugendamt in München  mit. Dort erledigte er vor allem \nBüroaufgaben, half bei Events  und in der  Hausaufgabenbetreuung einer offenen Einrichtung. Er \nfand die Arbeit so bereichernd, dass er seinen  Dienst von zwölf auf 18 Monate verlängerte. „Erst \nin dieser Zeit, in der ich mit Menschen zusammenarbeitete und ihnen half,  fand ich heraus, dass \nich Theologie studieren möchte – und nicht Informatik.“  Pro Monat bekam er 195 Euro \nTaschengeld, plus Pauscha len, wie etwa Fahrtkosten. Für ihn spielten die Finanzen allerdings \nkeine große Rolle, da er noch zuhause lebte.  Andreas empfand die Zeit als große persönliche \nBereicherung. „Es bringt den Freiwilligen total viel, und auch der Allgemeinheit. Es ist gut, um  \nsich in der Gesellschaft zu engagieren.“  \nGut findet Andreas auch, dass das Jahr eben freiwillig sei – dadurch könne man auch jederzeit \nabbrechen oder seinen Dienst  verkürzen, wenn man etwa ein Jobangebot  oder einen \nStudienplatz bekomme. So erklärt Andrea s sich auch die hohe Abbrecherquote. Er selbst kenne \njedoch nur einen Bufdi, der abgebrochen habe, und zwar deswegen, weil ihm eine Arbeitsstelle \nangeboten wurde.  \nAndreas beginnt nun bald sein Theologie Studium. Sein Fazit:  Ohne die Zeit als Bufdi  zwischen \nSchule und Studium hätte er seinen Weg dorthin womöglich nicht gefunden.  \nFrom: https://www.spiegel.de/politik/bundesfreiwilligendienst-bufdis-erzaehlen-weshalb-sie-abgebrochen-oder-\nverlaengert-haben-a-00000000-0003-0001-0000-000002419313 \n (501 words, adapted, last visited: February 06, 2023) \n \n(Inhalt ____ / 4 BE + Sprache ____ / 6 BE) x 2 = ______ / 20 BE \n \nTotal: ______ / 50 BE \nPart III: Sprachmittlung    20 BE"},"2024":{"year":2024,"listening":[{"title":"1. Name one reason for Biden’s decision.","items":[{"id":"1","label":"One reason:","key":"protection from major developments / keep places open for recreation","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"2. Avi Kwa Ame. Fill in the grid.","items":[{"id":"2a","label":"nickname:","key":"Spirit Mountain","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2b","label":"size (acres):","key":"(over) 450,000","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2c","label":"exact location:","key":"southern Nevada","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2d1","label":"First feature of the landscape:","key":"Zwei verschiedene aus: wide landscapes, desert, rocky area, mountains, forests, desert plants, vegetation.","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2d2","label":"Second feature of the landscape:","key":"Zweiter eigenständiger Aspekt aus: wide landscapes, desert, rocky area, mountains, forests, desert plants, vegetation.","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2e","label":"One more animal besides bighorn sheep:","key":"(desert) tortoise / (big) lizards","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"3. Complete the sentence.","items":[{"id":"3","label":"Avi Kwa Ame is important for tribal groups because …","key":"historical artifacts / location of origin or creation stories / sacred place","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"4. Castner Range. Fill in the missing information.","items":[{"id":"4a","label":"Up to the ___ it was used by the Army for shooting practice.","key":"1960s","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"4b","label":"The landscape is awesome because of all the ___ flowers.","key":"yellow / poppy","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"4c","label":"But otherwise, it’s a ___ landscape.","key":"desert","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"4d","label":"A great place for ___ , camping, and other outdoor recreation.","key":"hiking","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"5. Tick the correct statement about Castner Range.","items":[{"id":"5","label":"Select one statement.","type":"choice","options":["It is not yet safe to visit.","The Military is running camps there.","A study of plants is carried out there."],"correct":[0],"points":1}]},{"title":"6. Tick the two correct statements.","items":[{"id":"6","label":"President Biden …","type":"choice","options":["is very popular with climate activists.","has recently been criticized for his agreeing to whale fishing in Alaska.","wants to decide on another national monument in Colorado in October.","wants to declare a number of sites national monuments while president.","has been pushed by community groups to declare the two sites national monuments."],"correct":[3,4],"points":2}]}],"grammar":[{"id":"1","answers":["extremely"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Das Adjektiv happy wird vom Adverb extremely verstärkt.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"2","answers":["completing","she completed","she had completed","having completed"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"After + -ing oder after + vollständiger Nebensatz.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"3","answers":["getting"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Präposition of + -ing.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"4","answers":["was welcomed"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Simple past passive: was + welcomed.","topic":"passive","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"5","answers":["have never been","’ll never be","will never be"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Erfahrung bis zu diesem Moment: present perfect.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"6","answers":["putting"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Look forward to + -ing. Put verdoppelt t.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"7","answers":["scariest"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Scary → scarier → scariest.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"8","answers":["was travelling","was traveling"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Längerer Vorgang in der Vergangenheit: past progressive.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"9","answers":["would have","’d have"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Typ II: if + simple past → would + Grundform.","topic":"conditionals","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"10","answers":["✓"],"type":"correction","original":"tracked","explanation":"Die Reise wurde verfolgt: passive Partizipialkonstruktion.","topic":"participles","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"11","answers":["many"],"type":"correction","original":"much","explanation":"Countries ist zählbarer Plural.","topic":"quantifiers","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"12","answers":["let"],"type":"correction","original":"made","explanation":"Let = erlauben, make = dazu bringen/zwingen.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"13","answers":["on a plane every day"],"type":"correction","original":"every day on a plane","explanation":"Ort vor Zeit.","topic":"basics","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"14","answers":["to"],"type":"correction","original":"that I","explanation":"Want + to-infinitive, nicht want that I.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"15","answers":["lives"],"type":"correction","original":"life","explanation":"Mehrere Menschen mit eigenen Leben: distributiver Plural lives.","topic":"possessive","caseSensitive":false}],"passages":[{"kind":"gap","title":"Task 1","text":"Waving her arms in the air, teenage pilot Zara Rutherford was  {{1|extreme}} happy after  {{2|complete}} a solo around- the-world flying odyssey with the dream of  {{3|get}} into the record books. “It was very difficult but very rewarding,” confessed the 19- year-old Belgian- British sensation who became the youngest woman to fly all the way around the globe alone in a cockpit. She landed at an airfield outside the Belgian town of Kortrijk and  {{4|welcome}} by a crowd of journalists, wellwishers and family on January 20, 2022, just over five months after her departure on August 18, 2021. “It’s very strange being back here but I  {{5|never | be}} happier in all my life,” she told a media conference, adding that, after an epic journey with stops in almost 30 countries, she was looking forward to  {{6|put}} her feet up for a while in just one place. Ms Rutherford, whose parents are pilots, said Mongolia’s vast, frozen expanse was the  {{7|scary}} part of her whole journey: a place of overwhelming distance between towns, and where the temperature fell below minus 20°C. While she  {{8|travel}} without seeing anything human for hundreds and hundreds of kilometres, there was one constant thought in her head: “If the machine stopped, I  {{9|have}} a really big problem.”","offset":0},{"kind":"correction","title":"Task 2","text":"The 52,000km trip, {{10|tracked}} on her website so that everyone could see it, brought its share of unforgettable experiences. They included flying around the Statue of Liberty in New York and soaring above Saudi Arabia’s changing landscape. “I travelled through so {{11|much}} countries, but ev ery single one was amazing,” she said. “I’m so thankful that my parents said yes and {{12|made}} me go on this journey,” Ms Rutherford added. “Now that I’m back home, it’ ll be very strange not to get {{13|every day on a plane}} . But I’m just happy to finally be in the s ame spot for a few months.” Ms Rutherford told the reporters that she hoped to encourage more girls and women to go into flying. “I want {{14|that I}} inspire people to do something crazy with their {{15|life}}, to go for it,” she said.","offset":9}],"writingKey":"Folgende inhaltliche Punkte sollten in der Schülerarbeit (insg. ca. 170 Wörter) \nenthalten sein: \n \ndefinition of German “Abendbrot“:  \n• special form of evening meal: bread with slices of ham/sausage, cheese and \nvegetables \n• traditionally eaten between 5 and 7pm \n \norigin:  \n• since 1920s: more and more industrial workplaces   cooked lunch in factory \ncanteens  no wish for second cooked meal in the evenings (also due to less \nphysically demanding work)  “Abendbrot” \n• after the war: an increasing number of women employed   continuing upward \ntrend of fast and easy evening meal  \n \nreasons for recent decline:  \n• changing eating habits: trend towards low-carb dinners and other dishes \n• for 38% of the German population most important (warm) meal of the day \n \nconcept of “Abendbrot” will persist:  \n• older people and families don’t give up their “Abendbrot”  \n• also: revival of the German “Abendbrot” in some urban areas mainly due to \norganic bakeries \n \n \n4 BE","writingSource":"6 \nJahrgangsstufentest 10 Englisch 2024 – Gymnasium: Aufgaben \n \n \n  \n \nSie verbringen ein Jahr an einer englischen Schule.  In der Schülerzeitung sollen verschiedene \nArtikel zu typischen Bräuchen und Gewohnheiten der Herkunftsländer der Gastschülerinnen und \nGastschüler erscheinen.  \nBasierend auf dem vorliegenden Text schreiben Sie einen Artikel auf Englisch (ca. 170 Wörter) mit \neiner passenden Überschrift, in dem Sie das typisch deutsche Abendbrot beschreiben und seinen \nUrsprung darstellen. Gehen Sie anschließend auf die Gründe für den Trend weg vom typischen \nAbendbrot ein. Geben Sie abschließend wieder, warum dieser Brauch in Zukunft dennoch \nweiterleben wird. \n \nSchnittchendämmerung: Kommt der Untergang des Abendbrots? \n \n„Abendbrot\" wird in Deutschland oft das Abendessen genannt. Zu sich genommen wird es \nzwischen 17 und 19 Uhr. So erklären es auch Deutschlandreiseführer  oder Bücher über Deutsch \nals Fremdsprache. \nDoch in der modernen Arbeitswelt und der globalisierten Zeit des Low -Carb-Dinners – also der \nEmpfehlung vieler Ernährungsexperten, abends kohlenhydratarm zu essen – da stirbt die \nMahlzeit der Scheiben Brot mit Aufschnitt möglicherweise aus. Droht der Untergang des \nAbendbrots? Herrscht jetzt Schnittchendämmerung? \nDie Wurstindustrie gibt sich jedenfalls leicht alarmiert. Allerdings löst nicht etwa - wie man \ndenken könnte - das wachsende Angebot veganer und vegetarischer Fleischersatzprodukte \nSorgen aus. Folgenreicher sei der Wandel der Essgewohnheiten insgesamt. Statt Pausen-  oder \nAbendbrot mit Belag kämen zunehmend andere Gerichte auf die Teller.  \nDer deutsche Brauch, abends kalt zu ess en, stammt aus den 1920er Jahren. Damals dominierte \nmehr und mehr die Industrie den Alltag - im Gegensatz zu den landwirtschaftlicheren Strukturen \nin Staaten wie Italien und Frankreich. \nIn Fabriken gab es immer öfter Kantinen. Wer dort mittags speiste, wol lte abends oft kein \nwarmes Essen mehr. Da die Arbeit dank Technisierung auch körperlich weniger anstrengend \nwurde, liebten es viele am Abend leichter: Brot, Wurst, Käse, bisschen Rohkost, fertig. \nDas Abendbrot setzte sich dann nach dem Krieg noch stärker durch. Damals stieg auch die Zahl \nerwerbstätiger Frauen. Das schnell gemachte Abendbrot wurde Tradition in vielen Familien.  \nDennoch führen Millionen Deutsche heute ein Leben ohne abendliche Leberwurststulle. Die \nAllensbach-Studie „So is(s)t Deutschland\" im  Auftrag eines weltweit agierenden Nahrungsmittel -\nkonzerns förderte zutage, dass das warme Abendessen unter der Woche bei vielen inzwischen \ndie wichtigste Mahlzeit geworden ist. 2019 nannten 38 Prozent  der Deutschen das Abendessen \ndie Hauptmahlzeit des Tag es, zehn Jahre zuvor war en es noch deutlich weniger. Derzeit deutet \nalles darauf hin, dass der Megatrend zur warmen Hauptmahlzeit am Abend weitergeht.  \nDie Gießener  Künstlerin und Professorin Ingke Günther glaubt jedoch bei alledem nicht, dass \ndas früher populäre Abendbrot in Deutschland vollends verschwindet.  Es habe zwar seine \njahrzehntelang vorherrschende Rolle verloren, bei Älteren und in Familien mit Kindern ist das \nAbendbrot aber oft noch die Regel. Und in einigen städtischen Milieus, wo Bio- Bäckereien eine \nneue Brotkultur entwickelt haben, gebe es eine bewusste Rückbesinnung aufs Abendbrot.  Sie \nkommt zu dem Schluss: „Das Konzept, gemeinsam am Tisch zu sitzen und sich das Brot selbst \nzu belegen, ist einfach bestechend.  Die Bilder von einem gemeinsamen  Abendbrot sind in den \nKöpfen vieler Leute sehr lebendig.“ \nFrom: https://www.zeit.de/news/2022-03/10/schnittchendaemmerung-kommt-der-untergang-des-\nabendbrots?utm_referrer=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.de%2F \n (386 words, adapted, last visited: February 11, 2024) \n \n \n(Inhalt ____ / 4 BE + Sprache ____ / 6 BE) x 2 = ______ / 20 BE \n \nTotal: ______ / 50 BE \nPart III: Sprachmittlung    20 BE"},"2025":{"year":2025,"listening":[{"title":"1. Complete the table with facts on Gary Anderson.","items":[{"id":"1a","label":"year when he made his invention","key":"1970","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"1b","label":"his age at that time","key":"23","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"1c","label":"the subject he studied","key":"architecture","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"2. Fill in the missing information.","items":[{"id":"2a","label":"This all started when Gary Anderson saw ___ on campus.","key":"a poster / an ad","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"2b","label":"People were invited to put in ___ .","key":"(the design of) a logo for recycled paper products","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"3. Tick the two correct statements.","items":[{"id":"3","label":"Gary Anderson decided to take part because he …","type":"choice","options":["had just graduated from school.","had no special plans for his future.","had a great group of friends to support him.","had specific qualifications needed for this job.","had no need for any particular equipment to get the job done."],"correct":[1,4],"points":2}]},{"title":"4. Tick the correct statement.","items":[{"id":"4","label":"Select one statement.","type":"choice","options":["Gary Anderson didn’t even need a complete day for the major job.","Gary Anderson got his inspiration from maths classes at university.","Gary Anderson made use of recycled paper strips to complete the task."],"correct":[0],"points":1}]},{"title":"5. Tick the option that best represents Gary Anderson’s symbol.","items":[{"id":"5","label":"Select one picture.","type":"choice","options":["A · links","B · Mitte","C · rechts"],"correct":[2],"points":1}],"image":"material/2025-symbols.webp","alt":"Drei Symbolvarianten: links drei untereinander angeordnete gebogene Pfeile; Mitte zwei versetzt angeordnete Pfeile; rechts drei Pfeile in Dreiecksform."},{"title":"6. Complete the table with facts on what happened then.","items":[{"id":"6a","label":"what he got after a while","key":"a letter telling him he had won / confirmation that he had won","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"6b","label":"the sum of money he received ($)","key":"2,000","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"6c","label":"why he spent it for studying","key":"had to be used for / to further his education","type":"text","points":1},{"id":"6d","label":"which institution he went to","key":"University of Stockholm","type":"text","points":1}]},{"title":"7. Tick the correct option.","items":[{"id":"7","label":"Gary Anderson first saw his work in public on a trip to Amsterdam. It was on …","type":"choice","options":["A · links","B · Mitte","C · rechts"],"correct":[1],"points":1}],"image":"material/2025-containers.webp","alt":"Drei Behälter: links ein eckiger Altglascontainer; Mitte ein kuppelförmiger Sammelcontainer mit runder Öffnung; rechts ein öffentlicher Abfalleimer."},{"title":"8. Fill in the missing information.","items":[{"id":"8","label":"Seeing his work in public made him think people regarded the issue as a serious matter. He is especially proud that his work …","key":"raised awareness that things can be recycled / about recycling","type":"text","points":1}]}],"grammar":[{"id":"1","answers":["known"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Verkürzter passiver Relativsatz: known.","topic":"participles","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"2","answers":["to set"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Mean im Sinne von beabsichtigen verlangt to-infinitive.","topic":"ing","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"3","answers":["the longest"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Superlativ mit the: the longest.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"4","answers":["beating"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Aktive Bedeutung → present participle.","topic":"participles","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"5","answers":["greater"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"The + Komparativ … the + Komparativ.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"6","answers":["would help","could help","might help"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Backshift von will → would; could/might sind im Originalschlüssel ebenfalls zugelassen.","topic":"reported","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"7","answers":["was living"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Zeitgleich andauernder Vorgang in der Vergangenheit.","topic":"tenses","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"8","answers":["virtually"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Das Verb teaching wird durch ein Adverb beschrieben.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"9","answers":["had to"],"type":"gap","original":"","explanation":"Vergangenheit von must → had to.","topic":"modals","caseSensitive":false},{"id":"10","answers":["was finally able"],"type":"choice","original":"","explanation":"Fähigkeit/Möglichkeit, nicht Erlaubnis oder Pflicht.","topic":"modals","caseSensitive":false,"options":["was finally allowed","was finally able","finally had"]},{"id":"11","answers":["had changed"],"type":"choice","original":"","explanation":"Die Veränderung war vorher passiert: past perfect.","topic":"reported","caseSensitive":false,"options":["changed","has changed","had changed"]},{"id":"12","answers":["will help"],"type":"choice","original":"","explanation":"Direkte Aussage mit hope über eine zukünftige Wirkung.","topic":"future","caseSensitive":false,"options":["is going to help","will help","would help"]},{"id":"13","answers":["during"],"type":"choice","original":"","explanation":"Vor der Nomengruppe his time undersea: during.","topic":"prepositions","caseSensitive":false,"options":["while","during","when"]},{"id":"14","answers":["much"],"type":"choice","original":"","explanation":"Much verstärkt einen Komparativ; nicht more better.","topic":"comparison","caseSensitive":false,"options":["much","more","less"]},{"id":"15","answers":["scientists’"],"type":"choice","original":"","explanation":"Plural endet auf s → Apostroph hinter s.","topic":"possessive","caseSensitive":false,"options":["scientists","scientist’s","scientists’"]}],"passages":[{"kind":"gap","title":"Task 1","text":"Dr. Joseph Dituri, also  {{1|know}} as “Dr. Deep Sea”, has just finished an amazing adventure. The University of South Florida teacher spent 100 days living in a small room underwater. He meant  {{2|set}} a new world record for  {{3|long}} time spent underwat er, in this way  {{4|beat}} the old record of 73 days. Dr. Dituri’s goal was to stay for 100 days at a special underwater hotel called Jules’s Undersea Lodge, 22 feet under the ocean surface. “The further you go below the ocean’s surfac e, the  {{5|great}} the pressure,” explained Dr. Dituri. He wanted to learn how this pressure would affect the human body over time. He added that one day high pressure  {{6|help}} people live longer. All the time wh ile he  {{7|live}} underwater, Dr. Dituri stayed busy. He exercised in the morning and continued teaching his college students  {{8|virtual}}. Of course, he  {{9|must}} be checked by a medical team every week. The doctor even had a number of visitors who dove down to spend a little time with him.","offset":0},{"kind":"choice","title":"Task 2","text":"On June 9, Dr. Dituri returned to the surface where he {{10|choice}}to see the sun again. Doctors quickly checked him out to make sure he was okay. He then spoke to the crowd about his 100 days underwater. He said that this experience {{11|choice}}him in an important way and he added : “I hope my underwater research {{12|choice}}to treat a variety of illnesses eventually.” Actually, Dr. Dituri went through several big changes. For one thing, he became half an inch shorter {{13|choice}}his time in the high pressure undersea. He was also able to sleep {{14|choice}}better. Dr. Dituri and his team of doctors plan to study the information they collected in the course of the project. In November, the {{15|choice}}discoveries will be presented at an important medical meeting in Scotland.","offset":9}],"writingKey":"Folgende inhaltliche Punkte sollten in der Schülerarbeit (insg. ca. 170 Wörter) \nenthalten sein: \n \nThe Time Pyramid: \nproject:  \n• art project by Manfred Laber \n• construction began in 1993 on a hilltop in the north of Wemding (on the occasion \nof the town’s 1200th anniversary) \n• four stone blocks laid so far, one every 10 years \n• expected year of completion: 3183 (construction time: 1200 years) \n \nconcept:  \n• in total 120 huge stone blocks arranged as a pyramid  \n• one stone block is added every 10 years \n• (a model of the Time Pyramid can be viewed at the KunstMuseum Donau-Ries) \n \nfascination for viewers of the Time Pyramid:  \n• confronted with the passing of time (past – present – future)  \n• have to “endure” the fact that they do not know whether it will ever be completed \n• pass on an art project to future generations \n• (more of a social project than a mere art project) \n \n4 BE","writingSource":"6 \nJahrgangsstufentest 10 Englisch 2025 – Gymnasium: Aufgaben \n \n \n \n \nThe Museum of Modern Art in New York has an online platform called “Timeless Art”. This \nplatform has started a n initiative to find examples of pioneering art from all over the world. \nAs you are fascinated by Manfred Laber's Time Pyramid, you hand in an art icle for the \nonline platform in which you outline the project and the concept of the Time Pyramid as \nwell as the fascination it holds for its viewers. Don’t forget a headline and write about 170 \nwords.  \nAlles zu seiner Zeit \nDie Wemdinger Zeitpyramide wird voraussichtlich im Jahr 3183 fertiggestellt. Alle \nzehn Jahre soll die Pyramide um einen Stein erweitert werden. Bei einer \nVeranstaltung 30 Jahre nachdem der erste Steinquader mit einem Kran auf die \nWemdinger Platte gehoben wurde, kam nun der vierte Stein hinzu. \nWer dort oben auf der Wemdinger  Platte steht und die großen Steinquader betrachtet, setzt \nsich mit dem Lauf der Zeit auseinander. Wird dieses Kunstwerk jemals fertiggestellt? Wer \nstellt es fertig? Wie viele solcher Steinsetzungen werde ich selbst noch miterleben? Wer die \nSteinblöcke der Wemdinger Zeitpyramide betrachtet, blickt in die Vergangenheit und in die \nZukunft. \nAnlässlich der 1200- Jahrfeier der Stadt Wemding wurde 1993 mit dem Bau der Pyramide \nbegonnen, und genau 30 Jahre später wurde das Kunstprojekt auf einer Hügelkuppe am \nnördlichen Stadtrand Wemdings fortgeführt. \nDie Pyramide besteht aus 120 Steinquadern mit den Maßen 1,20 Meter x 1,20 Meter x 1,80 \nMeter. Die erste Ebene soll aus 8 x 8 Steinblöcken, also 64 Steinblöcken bestehen, die \nzweite Ebene aus 36 Blöcken, die dritte Ebene aus 16 und die vierte Ebene aus vier. Die 120 \nSteinblöcke insgesamt, die jeweils im Abstand von zehn Jahren gesetzt werden, \nentsprechen somit genau dem Zeitraum von 1200 Jahren.  \nDer Künstler Manfred Laber begann 1993 mit dem Bau der Pyramide. Er wurde 1 932 in \nWemding geboren und absolvierte ein Studium der Malerei an der Hochschule der Bildenden \nKünste in Berlin, wo er 1954 seine Meisterprüfung ablegte. Auch andere Kunstwerke ließ er \nbereits von Einflüssen der Zeit und der Natur, wie Sonne und Wind, gest alten, so zum \nBeispiel seine Skulpturen und Werke auf der Insel San Antoni in Spanien. Am 17. August \n2018 verstarb Manfred Laber im Alter von 86 Jahren in Wemding. Die Steinsetzung im \nSeptember 2023 war nun die erste, die der Künstler selbst nicht mehr mit erleben konnte. \nWie die Pyramide einmal aussehen soll, legte Laber in einem Modell, welches im \nKunstMuseum Donau-Ries zu besichtigen ist, fest. \nIn Wemding gibt es einen großen Freundeskreis der Zeitpyramide, der Labers Idee \nweiterträgt. Die 2003 gegründete Stiftung „Wemdinger Zeitpyramide“ hat sich zum Ziel \ngesetzt, die Errichtung der Pyramide nach dem Konzept Manfred Labers zu fördern. Vor \nallem die Auseinandersetzung mit der Zeit – mit Vergangenheit, Gegenwart und Zukunft – ist \nes, was die Stiftungsmitgli eder an der Zeitpyramide so fasziniert. Dazu gehört auch das \n„Aushalten“, weil man nicht sehen kann, nicht wissen kann, ob sie jemals fertiggestellt wird. \nAußerdem ist es ihnen wichtig, die Idee des Kunstprojekts der Zukunft an nachkommende \nGenerationen weiterzugeben. Letztendlich wird es dadurch vom Kunst - zum \nGesellschaftsprojekt. \nFrom: https://www.donau-ries-aktuell.de/das-beste-aus-dem-blaettle/alles-zur-seiner-zeit-wemding-78357 \n (414 words, adapted, last visited: February 18, 2025) \n(Inhalt ____ / 4 BE + Sprache ____ / 6 BE) x 2 = ______ / 20 BE \n \nTotal: ______ / 50 BE \nPart III: Sprachmittlung    20 BE"}};
